WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.489
An embedding maps text into a numeric vector so that a chosen

00:00:04.489 --> 00:00:06.359
similarity function can compare representations.

00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:11.477
Cosine similarity measures the angle between vectors; a dot product also depends

00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:13.961
on magnitude unless vectors are normalized.

00:00:14.111 --> 00:00:17.537
The meaning of distance depends on the embedding model and its training.

00:00:17.687 --> 00:00:21.151
Vectors from different models or dimensions are not interchangeable.

00:00:21.301 --> 00:00:26.175
Approximate nearest-neighbor indexes trade exactness for search speed and memory use.

00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:29.073
Evaluate their recall on representative data.

00:00:29.223 --> 00:00:34.400
Hybrid retrieval combines lexical and vector candidates, which can help with exact

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.557
identifiers as well as paraphrases.

00:00:36.707 --> 00:00:40.431
A reranker may improve final ordering but adds latency and cost.

00:00:40.581 --> 00:00:44.467
Access-control filters must remain effective throughout the pipeline.

00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:49.441
Retriever.cosine returns one for parallel nonzero vectors and zero for a zero

00:00:49.441 --> 00:00:51.452
vector in this teaching implementation.

00:00:51.602 --> 00:00:55.805
That arithmetic test does not demonstrate semantic search: the lab does not

00:00:55.805 --> 00:00:57.556
ship a pretrained embedding model.

00:00:57.706 --> 00:01:02.256
The extension replaces lexical scoring with real embeddings and compares both methods

00:01:02.256 --> 00:01:04.532
on the same labeled policy questions.

00:01:04.682 --> 00:01:08.081
Implement a vector-backed retriever using a local embedding model.

00:01:08.231 --> 00:01:13.353
Store model identifier, dimension, chunk version, and tenant with each vector.

00:01:13.503 --> 00:01:15.693
Rebuild the index when the model changes.

00:01:15.843 --> 00:01:20.550
Use actual embedding outputs rather than random vectors to claim semantic quality.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:26.424
Compare lexical recall, vector recall, hybrid recall, latency, and storage.

00:01:26.574 --> 00:01:29.639
Test wrong dimensions and cross-tenant near-duplicates.

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:33.794
A model migration normally needs separate indexes until the new corpus is

00:01:33.794 --> 00:01:34.128
ready.

00:01:34.278 --> 00:01:34.949
Question.

00:01:35.099 --> 00:01:37.494
Why can vector search miss a product code?

00:01:37.644 --> 00:01:38.220
Answer.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:43.504
Embedding similarity may not preserve exact string identity; lexical matching is often

00:01:43.504 --> 00:01:44.787
useful alongside it.

00:01:44.937 --> 00:01:45.608
Question.

00:01:45.758 --> 00:01:47.239
Does nearest mean relevant?

00:01:47.389 --> 00:01:47.965
Answer.

00:01:48.116 --> 00:01:48.599
No.

00:01:48.749 --> 00:01:53.164
An index always has a nearest candidate even when no candidate answers

00:01:53.164 --> 00:01:56.107
the question; use evaluation and an abstention policy.
